## 2. Evidence

**On the SFO precedent and its measurement problem.** SFO's Quiet Airport Program, launched around 2020, "successfully removed over 90 minutes of unnecessary daily noise and achieved a reduction of more than 40 percent in overall onsite noise" — a claim originated by the airport and repeated in trade press [Source: https://blog.atlasied.com/what-is-a-quiet-airport-how-smart-technology-improves-passenger-experience]. Consumer coverage has documented pushback: passengers in a connecting hub "may lose situational awareness" when announcements are removed, and "missing a flight is extremely risky considering layovers can be short" [Source: https://simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/]. No independent longitudinal peer-reviewed study of SFO's program outcomes has been identified for this brief — the 40%/90-minute figure appears only in program-owner and vendor communications.

**On London City's silent-terminal model.** London City "does not offer final call announcements and paging of late passengers" and directs customers to rely on information screens throughout the departure lounge, restaurants, and gate areas [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/silent-airport-policy]. The behavioral premise, documented in Smithsonian's coverage of the airport-announcement war, is that "frequent fliers tended to don headphones as soon as they reached the terminal … novice fliers were distracted by shops and restaurants" [Source: https://www.smithsonianmag.com/air-space-magazine/may-i-have-your-attention-180968115/]. That premise is a good argument for reducing frequency and improving zoning. It is not an argument for exporting the LCY model into a hub whose LCY-equivalent passenger — the London business flyer — is a fraction of IAD's traffic.

**On Schiphol's model and its accessibility trade.** Schiphol is a "silent airport" where "there are virtually no announcements and your flight information can always be seen on screens in the terminal" [Source: https://www.schiphol.nl/en/assistance/flying-for-the-deaf-and-blind/]. Schiphol's own accessibility guidance requires that assistance requests be submitted to the airline "at least 48 hours before departure." That level of pre-notification is culturally acceptable in the Netherlands and legally defensible under EU Regulation 1107/2006; it is not the ADA effective-communication standard MWAA must meet.

**On ADA obligations.** ADAAG requires both audible and visual alarms in emergency warning systems, and expressly warns that "audible alarms installed in corridors and lobbies can be heard in adjacent rooms but a visual signal can be observed only within the space it occupies" [Source: https://www.corada.com/documents/adaag-manual/4-28-3]. That is the technical reason a quieter PA and richer FIDS are not swappable at the code layer; they solve different problems for different people.

**On the intelligibility floor.** NFPA 72 requires that voice alarm systems deliver intelligible audio "along with visible notification information to serve hearing-impaired individuals and high-noise areas," with a minimum Speech Transmission Index typically targeted at 0.45 or higher on the CIS scale [Source: https://www.alertus.com/blog/nfpa-72-mass-notification-requirements-guide]. ACRP Report 175 — the standing US airport reference — recommends "a signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of 10 dB(A) and a minimum STI of 0.45 for public address broadcasts in pier-style departure lounges" [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/5]. These are floors the Authority already inherits by code and industry practice; a new MWAA standard adds compliance overhead more than it changes the design requirement itself.

**On the cost of getting acoustics wrong late — and, by implication, the cost of getting the standard wrong early.** An airport terminal case documented in the IEC 60268-16 practitioner literature required "$2.8 million in remediation, eight weeks of disruption, and immeasurable passenger frustration" after an acoustic consultant's STI 0.38 prediction was overruled by design [Source: https://acousplan.com/blog/iec-60268-16-sti-guide]. That case cuts both ways: it argues for early specification, but it also warns that a standard adopted prescriptively — and then reopened later because personal audio, spatial notification, or biometric wayfinding matured — creates the same class of retrofit cost.

**On digital exclusion.** Consumer reporting on the paper-boarding-pass wind-down flags that "digital boarding passes are useless if your phone is dead" and that "low-income passengers are more likely to have older phones or limited data plans" [Source: https://creators.yahoo.com/lifestyle/story/airlines-are-eliminating-paper-boarding-passes--what-happens-if-your-phone-dies-184202065.html]. Age Action and equivalent advocacy groups have publicly characterized digital-only pathways as "digital exclusion" [Source: https://www.teethgrinder.co.uk/the-digital-divide-will-traditional-passport-holders-be-left-behind/]. A quiet-by-design standard that promises "redundant digital and mobile channels" as the accessibility answer inherits this exposure.

**On the MWAA financial and political context.** MWAA planned approximately $5.5B in new debt from 2025 to 2028 with net revenue debt service coverage expected to fall from 1.63x in 2024 toward roughly 1.3x over the next five years [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md, §3]. The new 15-year airline use-and-lease took effect January 1, 2025 [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md, §4]. IAD's 2025 traffic — 29.01M total, 10.53M international — sits atop a terminal complex with the Saarinen main terminal (National Register, 1978) and a $20B-plus program announced July 29, 2026 [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md, §5–6]. DCA served 24.89M in 2025, down 5.4%, and hosts the operational and political overhang of the January 29, 2025 midair collision, with NTSB's final report published February 2026 [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md, §6, §8].

