## 3. Specific scenarios where the thesis is wrong

**Scenario A — irregular ops at DCA during a slot-recovery hour.** A late-afternoon ground stop cascades into rolling delays across the 10 additional beyond-perimeter slots awarded in December 2024. Gate reassignment, standby-list calling, and misconnect messaging spike simultaneously. A design standard that has capped ambient PA and pushed communication to FIDS and the airline app produces measurable passenger loss (missed rebooks, extended clear-out of gate lounges) because the airline's tenant PA is now the only high-throughput channel in a policy environment that discourages using it. The standard has moved risk from the design phase to the operating phase and made recovery worse.

**Scenario B — an evacuation in a partially renovated IAD concourse.** A standard adopted late in the Concourse C/D replacement forces mid-project changes to voice-alarm zoning and STI verification, tripping FAR Part 139 and NFPA 72 recertification, and prolongs a phased opening under airline pressure. The Board is asked to grant an exception; the exception becomes the pattern; the standard becomes advisory in fact if not in text.

**Scenario C — an ADA challenge to a "digital-first" backstop.** A visually and hearing-impaired traveler misses a gate change communicated only on FIDS in a low-lighting concourse and files a Part 27 complaint. Discovery pulls the Design Manual amendment and the pre-adoption memo. The Authority defends by citing Schiphol; opposing counsel notes that Schiphol pre-registers deaf and blind passengers 48 hours in advance and that no US airport can lawfully impose that.

**Scenario D — the standard is written for the 2028 passenger, but the 2036 passenger arrives with different technology.** Personal spatial-audio devices, mandatory app-based passenger notification through the airline of record, and biometric boarding push the "redundant channel" architecture into places the 2026 standard did not contemplate. What was a mandatory PA-zoning grid becomes a stranded requirement generating change orders on projects designed to satisfy it.

**Scenario E — airlines invoke MII on the tenant-audio clauses.** The Authority's ability to bind gate-podium PA, airline-club audio, and concession televisions runs into a majority-in-interest process that MWAA now must litigate mid-program. Cost-recovery categorization is opened. Airline consent conditions attach that dilute the standard into non-binding aspiration.

