## 4. Direct quotes and data points the strategist must address or concede

1. **"There are virtually no announcements and your flight information can always be seen on screens in the terminal."** — Schiphol, on its own silent-airport model. Concede: the peer models MWAA is being asked to emulate are, in text, more sweeping than the run prompt's careful "quiet ≠ fewer announcements" language, and the airport's public commitment gets read against that peer text, not against MWAA's internal nuance.

2. **SFO's Quiet Airport Program is credited with removing "over 90 minutes of unnecessary daily noise" and cutting onsite noise "by more than 40 percent"** — figures that trace to the program owner and appear in trade and consumer press without a published, replicable methodology [Source: https://blog.atlasied.com/what-is-a-quiet-airport-how-smart-technology-improves-passenger-experience]. Concede: the flagship US precedent is a marketing metric, and the final article must either replace it with measured data or stop leaning on it.

3. **ACRP Report 175 recommends STI ≥ 0.45 and SNR of 10 dB(A) for pier-style departure lounges** [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/5]. Concede: the intelligibility floor MWAA needs is already a published US baseline, so much of what a new standard would say is duplicative of guidance MWAA's Design Manual can adopt by reference in a single sentence.

4. **"Audible alarms installed in corridors and lobbies can be heard in adjacent rooms but a visual signal can be observed only within the space it occupies."** — ADAAG 4.28.3 [Source: https://www.corada.com/documents/adaag-manual/4-28-3]. Concede: the "redundancy" premise doesn't survive line-of-sight physics without more PA, not less, or without a much richer visual-signal buildout the standard's cost case has not yet defended.

5. **An airport terminal case incurred "$2.8 million in remediation, eight weeks of disruption, and immeasurable passenger frustration" after an as-built STI of 0.38 overrode a consultant's early prediction** [Source: https://acousplan.com/blog/iec-60268-16-sti-guide]. Concede: this is the strongest single argument **for** early specification — but it is an argument for embedding STI/SNR/RT metrics into the Design Manual by reference to existing ACRP and NFPA guidance, not for a bespoke MWAA quiet-by-design standard.

6. **MWAA's coverage is projected to compress from 1.63x in 2024 to roughly 1.3x over the next five years while approximately $5.5B in new debt phases in through 2028** [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md, §3]. Concede: the standard's implementation profile — commissioning cost, POE verification, tenant-audio remediation, exception processing — needs a written cost envelope before it lands at the Board, and the operator has not supplied one.

---

## 5. The concession that would make the thesis defensible

Replace *"a common quiet-by-design standard binding on both airports, adopted now"* with *"a two-part instrument: (a) a Design Manual reference-adoption of ACRP Report 175, IEC 60268-16, and NFPA 72 intelligibility floors; and (b) an operational-governance policy — announcement scripts, PA suppression zones, tenant TV mute defaults, gate podium volume caps — implementable under current tenant agreements and reversible at the COO level."* Keep the option to escalate (b) into a binding design standard **only** where operational discipline demonstrably fails a measured intelligibility, dwell-experience, or accessibility test at IAD or DCA specifically. That version of the thesis survives airline scrutiny, the coverage window, the ADA bar, the post-January-29 political environment, and technology drift. The version in the run prompt does not.
